These artifacts should not all be promoted to top-level pages merely because their content is important.

The correct test is:

# Does the subject deserve an independently addressable canonical knowledge object?
# Is the historical fragment merely one reusable expression of that object?
# Does another canonical page need to transclude exactly this unit?
# Would a heading/section preserve coherence better than a new page?
# Does independent provenance or semantic querying justify a subobject/subpage/template?

== What this evidence says about current Entity types ==

The V1 subject/composition corpus directly exercises or supports:

* '''Concept''' — Bioluminescent Organisms and definition fragments;
* '''Project''' — strongest current reading of Fluorescent Coral Reefs as the modeled effort;
* '''Method''' — Systems analysis;
* '''Protocol''' — peer review and validation procedures;
* '''Implementation''' — remains a legitimate distinction because V1 explicitly tried to separate implementation from project, even though Fluorescent Coral Reefs mixed the two;
* evidence/review structures — important, but not necessarily Entity types.

This evidence supports keeping the current base vocabulary open. It does '''not''' yet justify adding Theory, Model, Framework, Software, Species, Algorithm or other specialized types.

== What this evidence says about Book Matter ==

V1 proves three distinct composition patterns:

=== 1. Parent + subject-specific subpages ===
The two first-class subject candidates use parent pages to assemble specialized children.

=== 2. Generic transcludable topic sections ===
V1 contains generic Topic Section / Transcludable Content artifacts intended for reuse across contexts.

=== 3. Typed small fragments ===
Definition, methodology, protocol and evidence-summary fragments use small reusable units, frequently with <code>&lt;onlyinclude&gt;</code> and fragment metadata.

The mature V2 rule should not choose one pattern universally.

<blockquote>'''Use the smallest reusable unit that creates real composition value while preserving a coherent canonical reading surface.'''</blockquote>

That means:

* headings by default;
* subpages when a substantial unit has independent lifecycle/provenance/reuse needs;
* templates for repeated behavior/presentation rather than ordinary prose;
* semantic subobjects where structured nested data is required;
* transclusion where one canonical body must appear in multiple contexts;
* top-level pages where the knowledge object is independently meaningful.

== Accepted migration decisions ==

# Do not recreate <code>Idea</code> as a V2 type merely to preserve its V1 label; <code>Concept</code> is the mature broad mapping unless the corpus later proves a distinct job.
# Preserve Project and Implementation as separate possible jobs; do not encode project lifecycle as epistemic standing.
# Preserve coherent parent pages even when their internal content is modular.
# Treat V1 fragment metadata as composition evidence, not as automatic new V2 properties.
# Revalidate historical statuses and factual claims before publishing subject canon.
# Preserve exact V1 bodies/revisions as provenance even when V2 chooses a different composition structure.

== Remaining canonicalization gate ==

Before either historical subject is published as a current V2 Main page:

# compare all preserved revisions;
# reconcile duplicate/parallel child units;
# recover and verify cited sources;
# separate established biological facts from project/speculative claims;
# preserve distinctive authored formulations that remain useful;
# assign explicit Entity type, Domain, epistemic standing and provenance without defaults;
# decide relationships from the modeled objects, not old label names;
# validate links/transclusions/semantic values.

This is a deliberate gate, not unfinished migration bookkeeping.

